\description{
Fits the abundance-induced heterogeneity model of Royle and Nichols (2003)
with false positives in Stan. The number of individuals at site \eqn{i} is
\eqn{N_i \sim \mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_i)}, each detected on an occasion
with probability \eqn{r}, so the automated method detects the species with
probability \eqn{1 - (1 - r)^{N_i}}, and the site is occupied when
\eqn{N_i > 0}. A site effect on \eqn{r} (\code{random_det}) then measures
variation in detection among sites beyond that due to abundance, which
\code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}} cannot separate from it.
}
\details{
\strong{Model.} With \eqn{N_i \sim \mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_i)},
\deqn{\log \lambda_i = X_i \beta \; [+ v_i],}
an automated occasion \eqn{k} of site \eqn{i} is a detection with
probability \deqn{1 - (1 - r_{ik})^{N_i} (1 - p_{10,i}),}
\deqn{\mathrm{logit}(r_{ik}) = Z_{ik} \alpha + u_{\mathrm{rec}[ik]} \; [+ d_i],}
\deqn{\mathrm{logit}(p_{10,i}) = \gamma_0 + X^{fp}_i \gamma \; [+ e_i],}
so false positives occur on every occasion, also at occupied sites, and an
unoccupied site (\eqn{N_i = 0}) is detected with probability
\eqn{p_{10,i}}. A verified occasion is a detection with probability
\eqn{1 - (1 - r^{v})^{N_i}}. The site effects \eqn{v_i}, \eqn{d_i} and
\eqn{e_i} are normal with standard deviations \code{sd_abund_site},
\code{sd_det_site} and \code{sd_fp_site} and half-normal priors (\code{abund_site_sd},
\code{det_site_sd} and \code{fp_site_sd} of \code{\link[=fp_priors]{fp_priors()}}). Unlike \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}}, the
false-positive intercept \eqn{\gamma_0} is not tied to the detection
intercept by a gap: detection at an occupied site always exceeds
\eqn{p_{10}}, so the labels cannot switch, and \eqn{p_{10}} may exceed
the per-individual detection \eqn{r}. Its prior is the marginal prior the
gap implies in \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}}: Normal with mean \code{-gap_mean} and standard
deviation \code{sqrt(intercept_sd^2 + gap_sd^2)}. \eqn{N_i} is summed out
over 0 to \code{n_max}.

\strong{Observation-confirmation design.} Confirmed occasions (argument
\code{confirmed} of \code{\link[=fp_data]{fp_data()}}) enter as in \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}}, with probabilities of
at least one true observation \eqn{s_1 = 1 - (1 - r)^{N_i}} and at least
one false observation \eqn{s_0 = p_{10}}. They can be the same occasions
as the automated detections, so a fully annotated survey enters as one
row per occasion with both columns filled.

\strong{Outputs.} \code{print()}, \code{summary()}, \code{as_draws_df()}, \code{\link[=loo]{loo()}} (leave
one site out), \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} (new abundances and detections with the
design of the data) and \code{\link[=fp_sites]{fp_sites()}} and \code{\link[=fp_ppc]{fp_ppc()}}, which use the
posterior of each site's abundance given its data; see
\link{fp_occu_rn-methods}.

\strong{Not supported.} False-positive covariates measured on each occasion,
runs of detections (\code{markov} of \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}}), and the other outputs of
\code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}} (\code{predict()}, \code{coef()}, \code{\link[=fp_effects]{fp_effects()}}, \code{\link[=plot]{plot()}}).

\strong{Identifiability.} In simulations (\code{vignette("royle-nichols")}, code
in \verb{dev/rn-fp/} of the package repository), the power to detect
\code{sd_det_site = 1} grew with the number of sites and occasions, from about
half at 25 sites and 10 occasions to above 90\% at 100 sites and 20
occasions, and depended little on whether verified data or confirmed
occasions supplied the information. \code{sd_abund_site} was weakly
identified. With few sites the prior of \code{sd_det_site} carries much of the
posterior near 0: compare fits under several values of \code{det_site_sd}.
The default \code{adapt_delta} is 0.99, above the 0.95 of \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}}: the
site effects create funnels, and at 0.95 most simulated designs had
divergences. With many sites, also use longer chains than the default:
the standard deviations of the site effects mix slowly.
}
